  "summary": "Cell-type-resolved profiling of FMRP loss, 22q11.2 deletion neurons and SETX knockout motor neurons refines disease mechanisms.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread across these preprints is cell-type-resolved analysis of how disease-linked perturbations reshape neural gene and protein programs. Cortical excitatory and inhibitory synapses from Fmr1-deficient mice show cell type-specific remodeling of mRNA and protein profiles after FMRP loss. Integrating bulk RNA-seq and single-nucleus multiome data from 231 human prefrontal cortices maps cell-type and developmental genetic regulation relevant to brain disorders. In iPSC-derived neurons from 22q11.2 deletion carriers, impaired mitophagy and lysosomal acidity are associated with schizophrenia, and the impairment is reversible. Clozapine and LDLR repression induce shared astrocytic cholesterol-pathway transcription in human neural cultures, linked to common genetic variation. SETX knockout in human motor neurons impairs neurite growth, synaptic activity and survival in a stage-dependent manner. What is new is the resolution: effects are assigned to specific cell types, developmental stages and reversible cellular processes rather than to bulk tissue.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-19"
